From b1bd6249661d08293289dec650e58cdd85a5c067 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 09:41:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] macOS: find recollindex when launched from recoll.app,
 and fix the dead menu bar for real

Two problems seen on both 10.6/PowerPC and 10.15/x86 with the Qt4 build:

1. "Indexing failed" immediately when the GUI is started from the recoll.app
   bundle (works when /opt/local/bin/recoll is run from a shell).

   The bundle produced by qmake contains a *copy* of the recoll binary in
   recoll.app/Contents/MacOS, so path_thisexecdir()/recollindex (the only
   place the GUI looked) does not exist and the exec fails. Launchd also gives
   the bundle a minimal PATH without the package manager's bin directory.

   - qtgui/rclm_idx.cpp: findRecollindex() tries, in order, next to our own
     executable, <PREFIX>/bin (qmake already defines PREFIX for the meson
     build), then the PATH, and logs an error if none works.
   - common/rclinit.cpp: on macOS, also prepend <prefix>/bin (derived from the
     compiled-in RECOLL_DATADIR, i.e. <prefix>/share/recoll) to the PATH, so
     the helper programs (python3, pdftotext...) are found from a bundle
     launch regardless of whether MACPORTS/HOMEBREW is defined.

2. Menu bar (and menu key equivalents such as Cmd+Q) unresponsive until the
   user switches to another application and back, when recoll is started from
   a shell. The previous fix (activateIgnoringOtherApps: right after
   QApplication construction) did not work because on Cocoa the app has not
   finished launching at that point: NSApplication's finishLaunching only runs
   from [NSApp run], which Qt4's event dispatcher calls from inside
   QApplication::exec(), and the activation done before that is lost. Qt4's
   qt_init() only does TransformProcessType() and never activates; Qt5's Cocoa
   plugin activates from applicationDidFinishLaunching, which is what works.

   qtgui/macactivateapp.mm now defers the activation: on Cocoa it observes
   NSApplicationDidFinishLaunchingNotification (Qt4's own delegate stays in
   place), and on both backends a moc-less QObject timer activates again once
   the Qt event loop runs (at 0 and 300 ms), which covers Carbon's
   SetFrontProcess() and acts as a retry on Cocoa.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016H46BgfYXKFbsVGPax89bu
